<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boris and Gleb</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1015</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Saints Boris and Gleb</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boris and Gleb were sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> who were murdered in 1015 during the succession struggle after their father's death, a crime the chronicles blame on their brother Sviatopolk. Their acceptance of death without resistance led to their veneration as passion-bearers, and they became the first saints canonised in Rus'. Gleb had held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murom">Murom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boris-and-gleb.jpg" alt="Boris and Gleb" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boris_Savvin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
